nt french  presidency it has managed to complete a  number of proposals successfully it was  not easy to find a compromise in  such a difficult  debate like the 50  for a fit 455 package  in the  area of climate  or  on new rules for the digital economy but  the most important thing that was  achieved during the french presidency  was a united action  to response in response to russia's  aggression against ukraine  our response was quick  it was tough  and  most importantly it was united  six packages of sanctions were not  approved easily a number of member  states had to give up their positions  some of their economic interests and  some of their comfort  but because we were convinced that it  was right to stand up to russia and to  help our friend and ally  i'm in ukraine we did it  together we also granted candidate  status to ukraine and moldova  it gave them hope  and faith in the future  and in joining free democratic europe  our presidency wants to build on this  work  and now  according to the european parliament  tradition  allow me to present the main priorities  of our presidency in my mother language  in check  ladies and gentlemen the topics and  priorities that we want to focus on  have been created over  a period of many months but 24th  february 2022 turned most of our plans  upside down literally  our priorities  reflect this new situation  we are in a time  where war is being waged just outside  the eu's borders this is a classic  conventional conflict many of us thought  was a thing of the past in europe  but apart from conventional war  russia is also waging hybrid war  intentionally  sparing a refugee wave  using energy resources as a weapon  and  by target and infrastructure it is  spurring a food crisis  from our point of view it is very good  that our membership in the eu and in  nato protects us from the most tragic  consequences  that that are being faced by the  ukrainian people  when on the 15th of march together with  the polish and slovenian prime ministers  i visited president zielenski in kiev  i saw with my very own eyes the heroism  of ukrainians who were and still are  fighting for their right to exist and  for their right to belong to western  civilized countries  this reminded me that our values were  not a matter of course that they need to  be defended day to day  or not  the values  for which ukrainians are dying these  days are values that were defended by  one of the founders of the modern czech  state  the president vars love havel it was his  inspiring essay from 1996  brought us to the motto of our  presidency  which goes europe as a task  and indeed  europe has many tasks before it  and similarly  the czech presidency of the council has  many tasks before it  the main task for the next months will  be to find a united and strong agreement  on such measures  that will dampen as much as possible the  most negative consequences of the  current crises on our citizens because  inflation unavailability of energy or  the food crisis are  threats that need to be faced and these  threats are best faced together i  believe that both the council and the  european parliament can agree on this  europe as a task is a commitment for us  it is not a given  it is something that we will need to  work on constantly that we will need to  perfect  also we need to adjust those things that  have not been working as planned  europe needs to lead by example  it needs to reflect its conscience and  it needs to face its challenges  our solutions however need to be  balanced so that  too much focus on one  does not damage or neglect another for  example social peace or our economy  the czech republic will always  very delicately try to reflect all  opinions that will be voiced in our  debates  we have to prevent  a split among us we always have to try  to find the  widest most possible agreement we have  to respect the opinions and position of  others even though we may not agree with  them  havel's words on europe as a task come  of course from another time they come  from another europe than what we have  today but their basic message remains  the same and as  strong  our motto is to rethink rebuild and  repower that reflects our challenges and  the problems of the current europe and  our reactions to them  the czech presidency will want to build  a safe and prosperous eu  such an eu that will be true to its  values of freedom and democracy an eu  that will enable all of its citizens to  travel and work freely or become engaged  in the internal market an eu which  understands that we do have an up  responsibility towards the environment  that we need to hand over to the next  generation  um  as as it is and an eu that builds on  what unites us and respects what makes  us diverse that's the eu that we want  and  in terms of this target we formulated  five pillars of the czech presidency  which i would now like to introduce to  you  the first priority  is managing the refugee crisis and a  post-war reconstruction of ukraine  the russian aggression directly or  indirectly is impacting all member  states of the eu the direct consequences  include the most massive refugee wave  since the second world war the czech  republic is one of the countries with  the highest number of ukrainian refugees  per capita  there are more than 3.5 percent refugees  in our population now  we do have our bitter experience with  russian imperialism we know what  aggressive russian policy can do the  czech republic is therefore convinced  that  political and military support of  ukraine from the eu  is in the utmost interest of the entire  eu  with direct impact on next future of our  continent  the czech presidency will therefore  defend and support the sovereignty and  territorial integrity of ukraine  using all tools at our disposal  we will focus on a strong and united  stance against russia  as well as the maximum possible  assistance to member states when  providing military support to ukraine  or when compensating for the  consequences of the migration wave  it is also very important to look in the  future  at the last european council we've  managed to approve the candidate status  for ukraine and moldova i'm very happy  that in this regard we had massive  support from the european parliament as  well i would like to use this  opportunity to thank you for that  but that must not be the end  another very important task of the czech  presidency will be preparing for a  post-war reconstruction of ukraine we  have to focus mainly on  reconstructing their infrastructure  providing basic services  their economic stability and recovery  that is where the eu must play a  fundamental role after all we discussed  this with madame fonda lyon  and we've demonstrated this on monday at  the conference  on the reconstruction of ukraine in  switzerland  the way of ukraine to the eu will still  be long  but together we need to strive that this  be a successful way that this make the  eu stronger  the second priority is energy security  the current crisis has fully shown us  how much the future of the eu is  existentially dependent  on others and how important it is to be  independent especially as far as  countries  which are threatening our safety are  concerned i'm talking about russian gas  oil and coal of course the way of the  czech presidency will be  the way of common european projects  which will rid us of our dependence on  russia  the czech presidency will focus  on issues of energy security  to make sure that we can implement  re-power eu as fast as possible and that  includes our energy resource  diversification we are ready to work on  coordinating gas storage facilities  before the winter we support  voluntary common acquisitions  according to the model that we've used  during the covet crisis  while doing all that we have to remember  that we may find ourselves in a  situation where more than ever it will  be necessary to show solidarity between  and among member states  we must not forget  building energy resilience of the eu for  the future through developing our energy  infrastructure at the same time we need  to support  all  eu  emission-free energy sources because  these play a very important role when  providing energy security in the eu as  well as in meeting our climate goals  the long-term goal of decarbonizing the  european economy  goes to a great extent hand-in-hand  with a short-term  goal to de-russify our energy supplies  and that's  that must be our viewpoint  in this regard to become energy  independent on russia we have to use all  options that are allowed by our  geographic situation given the size of  the eu and the number of member states  we may not apply a one-size-fits-all  approach every member state needs to  have the opportunity to choose such an  energy mix that is the most suitable for  their particular conditions and using  which they will be able to meet both the  climate climate targets as well as the  target of becoming independent on energy  supply from russia  if we indeed want to meet our climate  goals we have to be able to  finance them in the individual countries  i'm mentioning this because today ladies  and gentlemen you will be voting  on the objection against the so-called  taxonomy as introduced by the european  commission i would like to stress  that this is a very fragile proposal it  is a balanced proposal  a proposal that  makes it possible for a number of  countries to meet their commitments  stemming from our climate goals  only  thanks to criteria that were presented  in the proposal therefore i would like  to ask you  to  not reject this very fragile compromise  nuclear energy and gas as a temporary  energy source from safe countries  are the only tool that many countries  have at hand  in order  to  achieve our common climate goals in the  next years  the war in europe has shown how  important it is to boost european  defense capabilities as well as the  safety and security of european  cyberspace that's our third priority  given the current geopolitical situation  it is of key importance that we boost  european safety and security and defense  capabilities nato is in this matter and  natural partner to the eu and the  implementation of the key issues within  the strategic compass is the appropriate  framework we shall work on boosting  european cooperation in um boosting um  defense capabilities it is of urgent  importance to top up the military  warehouse provisions together with that  member states have provided to the  ukraine we also consider it of great  importance to  develop our defense industry and reduce  our dependence on  not  trustworthy countries in the long term  we shall work  on  facilitating joint purchasing of  military material  we are also facing new types of threats  that are being used by our enemies we  shall therefore focus on cyber threats  and making europe more resilient um  vis-a-vis outside threats such as fights  against disinformation the safety of the  cyberspace as well as key eu  infrastructure protection including  institutions  protection and that's what we have to do  together with our partners and allies  our fourth priority is strategic  resilience of european economy  it was the kovich pandemic that has  shown some weak points in european  economy one-sided dependency on some  countries  europe must  be  indus an industrial and technology  leader  for that it is important to have a  well-functioning  single market without unnecessary  obstacles only that will boost  our european companies  we therefore have to understand the  supply chains better  and make them as short as possible so  that the availability of strategic  resources and spare parts is always a  matter of certainty  what plays a key role here is boosting  free trade with other partners who share  our values the more free trade  agreements of quality we have the more  we can diversify  our contacts and supply chains what i  consider important is also the  digitization and automation in european  industry which also must lead in  innovation and new technologies as far  as strategic resilience of european  economies concerned  food security is of utmost importance  europe has become self-sufficient  in food however the war in the ukraine  also has its implications on food  security and the european union must  respond  we should not forget about the  importance of providing  food  in all parts of the world including the  most vulnerable ones not only because  destabilizing in the food area would  represent  a threat  to europe ronald reagan said that  freedom is never more than one  generation away from extinction  the same can be said about democracy  that is why  it is so important to have resilient  democratic institutions  and that is our fifth and last priority  long-term prosperity and stability of  europe  is based on functional democratic  mechanisms  our society is our  the targets of attacks  by those who have a problem with  democracy with human rights etc and we  must defend those values that is why we  shall always support  and boost  the freedoms fundamental human rights  civil society free media as well as  independent institutions  across europe  ladies and gentlemen there is no doubt  that  um we are facing a difficult time not  only due to the russian aggression in  the ukraine we are fighting increasing  inflation ever increasing energy prices  in some countries rising unemployment  in some countries we're facing the drop  in the gdp  we should not  therefore forget about those  citizens who are bearing the brunt of  this socially we must not  uh forget about those in need i can  reassure you  that  those are issues  that we shall focus on in a systematic  way  ladies and gentlemen  the six-month presidency is indeed a  short time  however i can assure you that the czech  presidency is ready to take its role  actively we are ready to focus  on an open dialogue to seek compromises  and functional solutions in cooperation  with other eu institutions  i believe that we will be able to  deliver our promises i hope that with  our help ukraine will soon be on the way  to full recovery i also hope that we  will be successful in the fight against  inflation  in ensuring energy security employment  and increasing the competitiveness of  our economies  i trust that we will be even stronger  than we are now  ladies and gentlemen i am convinced that  we can count  on  help on the help of the european  parliament which is a pillar of european  democracy  we highly value  democratic divides debates on this floor  and i'm looking for forward for  our debate thank you very much for your  attention  thank you so much  prime minister  for that comprehensive overview and  introduction i give the floor now to the  president of the european commission  ursula wonderland  viendank  thank you  president mrs matsuola dear roberta  prime minister dear peter  ladies and gentlemen honorable members  we  can all bring to mind the images of  russia's invasion of ukraine when it  started  ukrainian citizens where had no weapons  and they stood tall against the russian  soldiers spoke with the soldiers shouted  at them  flagged quite frankly how absurd the war  was  those images  remind us  of a different invasion and another  example of resistance against oppression  in prague in 68  we saw unforgettable scenes of peaceful  demonstrators taking to the streets  of prague and standing firm against  soviet tanks  and so i cannot imagine  a better country one more suited to  taking over the rotating presidency of  the council in the european union no  better country than the czech republic  the czech people have suffered under  oppression and the people in the czech  republic  know all about resistance the power of  hope  and the joy that comes with free  democracy  now more than ever  we need to strive for freedom  self-determination in the european union  so my dear peter i look forward to the  czech presidency of the european union  over the next six months  are enormous  you better have sum them up with three  words  rethink re-power rebuild  and of course the word rebuild calls to  mind first and foremost the  reconstruction of ukraine  i've just returned where i met you also  peter from the lugano reconstruction  conference  and i think we witnessed a very  impressive presentation  of ukraine's reconstruction plan  representatives from  more than over 40 countries and  organizations agreed on the lugano  principles  ukraine owned  reform focused  transparency  accountability and rule of law  democratic participation  gender equality and inclusion  and sustainability and i think these are  good principles now we have to fill them  with life  and for doing that we need the best and  the brightest on reconstruction  and this is why together with the german  g7 presidency  we will convene such a high level  conference after summer because we all  know never ever before have we mastered  such a colossal reconstruction challenge  so we need all hands on deck  and while we work in these days to help  ukraine win this war  we must also make sure that ukraine wins  the peace that will for sure come  you honorable members  were the first to call for the european  perspective for ukraine  and this led to the historic decision to  grant ukraine candidate status  i think we can all take pride in this  because our union lived up to the moment  we did exactly what vaclav havel  reminded us of  in his speech which also provides the  motto for your presidency  he has said  it is not  that the united part of europe would  come to harm if it expanded  just the opposite  in the long run  it would come to harm only  if it failed to expand  and i think in these days  that really sounds like a prophecy  now the second task you the czech  presidency identified is repower  the unity and the resolve we show  towards russia will also be key to  re-power europe  and this means primarily  to push forward the very ambitious  agenda of our european green deal  because climate change will not wait  till putin's war is over  and the unprecedented heat wave that  recently struck europe is just the  latest reminder of that  now i'm very glad that both parliament  and council  have taken their positions on fit for 55  so now it's up to the czech presidency  to bring forward the tri-logs  and i'm also very grateful for your  commitment dear peter to bring forward  the re-power eu package  with 3power eu we are mobilizing  substantial substantive new resources  for renewable energy  it is a 300 billion euro plan  to win independence from russian fossil  fuels actually from any fossil fuels  and to do so  we are first and foremost diversifying  our gas supplies away from russia  towards trustworthy reliable suppliers  and our efforts are already making a big  difference i have reported several times  here in the parliament about that so let  me update you about what the latest  efforts are  since march global lng exports  other than russia  to europe have risen by 75 percent  compared to 2021  and lng exports from the united states  to the european union have nearly  tripled  i think even more important is  that at the same time  the average monthly import of russian  pipeline gas to the european union  is now sharply declining by 33  compared to last year  last year june last this year june minus  33  so we are making progress and that's  exactly what we wanted to have  but we know that all of this will only  work the diversifying away from russia  and having energy security of supply if  we speed up the transition to renewable  energies this is the key remedy  now i observe that some say  in the new security environment after  russia's aggression  we have to slow down  the green transition  and this transition would come at the  cost of basic security they say  just the opposite is true  if we do all nothing but compete around  fossil fuels the limited fossil fuels  that are out there the prices will just  go through the roof and we will fill  putin's war chest and that's exactly  what we do not want to have  and therefore the best the cleanest and  safest way out of the dependency of  fossil fuels are renewables  so  the new security environment  is the best argument to speed up  deployment of renewables  with a home grown  they give us independent from russian  fossil fuels  they are more cost efficient  and they are cleaner than anything else  so let's take the opportunity and really  invest in the renewable energy  honorable members  but we also need to prepare now  for further disruption of gas supply and  even a complete cut up of of russian gas  supply  just to give you  a figure today  12 member states already directly  affected  by partial or total  cutoff of russian gas  so it is obvious  putin continues to use energy as a  weapon  and this is why  the commission is working on a european  emergency plan  we will present this plan and the  necessary instruments by mid-july  member states have their national  contingency plans that's good  but i think we need european cooperation  and we need common action as 27.  we need to make sure that in case of  full disruption  the gas flows towards where it's needed  most  we have to provide for european  solidarity  and we need to protect the single market  as well as industry supply chains  and here honorable members  we should never forget the bitter bitter  lesson that we learned at the beginning  of the pandemic you remember it very  well at the beginning we had egoism and  protectionism close borders export bans  you name it that led only to disunity  and fragmentation  but with european unity and coordination  we then after a while had the key  remedies to overcome this brutal  pandemic  so let's keep these principles in mind  as always let's hope for the best but  prepare for the worst i know this is  hard work ahead of us  but like in the last month unity will be  key to our success  madame  deputy  honorable members  you know  as well as i do  that in all member states  there are worrying economic trends the  covert crisis  has deepened debt levels and just when  economies were starting to rebound  russia began a ferocious attack against  ukraine this accelerated inflation in  all  sectors  the food sector the energy sector and  all in between  at the same time investment is key  to ensure a transition a green  digital resilient transition and that is  why we need to rethink  rethink my dear peter  is part of your motto and it is very  appropriate  we need to rethink the way  we drive growth  we have a very different political and  economic context we know the rules of  economic governance  we need  rules that recognize our need for  investment and the need for healthy  budgets  the budgetary  viability  and indeed growth go hand in hand  we will present the results of our  assessment of economic governance during  the czech presidency look at the single  market  you talked about this  you talked about our trade relationship  these are two major assets in tackling a  crisis the main drivers of growth  we need to rethink the way we can  strengthen those assets further we need  to look at new  free trade agreements one for example  signed last week with new zealand that  is a good case in point  the new agreement will help to bring  about a 30  increase in trade between the european  union and new zealand  for the first time  it enshrines unprecedented commitments  on social and climate issues  this shows  that when democracies are strong they  can deliver results in the interests of  their citizens in his  speech  vats love harvard spoke about  europe as a task  the task of europe today he said  is to rediscover its conscience and its  responsibility it's beautiful and of  course  and this is exactly what we will  continue to do with the support of our  czech friends 